From a42acfe2ad087c4864e19f689b9fe939656d6db7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:54:35 +0000
Subject: ipa: softisp: agc: Lengthen the frame for longer exposures

The exposure is limited to the sensor's frame at its default vertical
blanking (about 33 ms), where the tuning's exposure table reaches 70 ms.
With a maxExposure in the tuning, once the exposure is at its limit and the
gain a stop above unity, raise the vertical blanking for more exposure time
rather than gain, up to the tuning's longest, and shorten the frame again
as the scene brightens.

The tuning's exposure table keeps the exposure near the frame's 33 ms until
the gain is about 32, and reaches 50 to 70 ms at 64 and above. Lengthening
the frame as soon as the exposure ran out dropped the frame rate to 14 fps
in a merely dim room. Allow the exposure the table has at the gain, and
shorten it first when the gain falls.

The tuning's exposure table goes on to more gain than the sensor's analogue
gain, and to longer exposures with it. Where the gain is at its maximum
there is none left to raise, so the exposure takes the rest of the way.

When the scene brightens within the exposure's tolerance nothing moved the
exposure, which stayed longer than the tuning has at its gain, and the
frame stayed long. Trade the excess for gain at the same brightness and
shorten the frame to it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
